---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

// Static docs — one page with a sticky section nav (Wealthfolio-inspired
// Concepts vs. User Guide split, scaled to our page count).
const sections = [
  {
    group: 'Getting Started',
    items: [
      { id: 'what-is-this', label: 'What is WealthFlow?' },
      { id: 'first-upload', label: 'Your First Upload' },
    ],
  },
  {
    group: 'Concepts',
    items: [
      { id: 'cost-basis', label: 'Cost Basis & P&L' },
      { id: 'rebalance-drift', label: 'Rebalance Drift' },
      { id: 'benchmarks', label: 'Benchmark Comparison' },
    ],
  },
  {
    group: 'User Guide',
    items: [
      { id: 'upload-format', label: 'CSV / Sheet Format' },
      { id: 'dividends', label: 'Recording Dividends' },
      { id: 'goal-planner', label: 'Goal Planner' },
      { id: 'privacy-mode', label: 'Privacy Mode' },
      { id: 'themes', label: 'Themes' },
    ],
  },
  {
    group: 'Reference',
    items: [
      { id: 'price-providers', label: 'Price Providers' },
      { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQ' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout title="Docs & Guide" description="How to use the WealthFlow portfolio tracker: uploads, concepts, and reference.">
  <div class="w-full max-w-[1600px] mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-10 2xl:px-16 py-8">
    <div class="mb-8 flex items-center justify-between">
      <div>
        <h1 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl">Docs &amp; Guide</h1>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mt-1">Everything you need to run your portfolio in WealthFlow.</p>
      </div>
      <a href="/" class="text-xs font-bold text-accent border border-accent/30 rounded-lg px-3 py-2 hover:bg-accent/10 transition">
        &larr; Back to Dashboard
      </a>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-[240px_1fr] gap-8 lg:gap-12 items-start">
      <!-- Section nav -->
      <nav class="md:sticky md:top-6 space-y-5 text-sm">
        {sections.map((section) => (
          <div>
            <div class="text-[10px] uppercase font-bold text-slate-500 tracking-wider mb-1.5">{section.group}</div>
            <ul class="space-y-0.5">
              {section.items.map((item) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={`#${item.id}`} class="sidebar-link !py-1.5 text-xs">{item.label}</a>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        ))}
      </nav>

      <!-- Content -->
      <div class="xl:columns-2 xl:gap-8 text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed [&_h2]:text-lg [&_h2]:mb-2 [&_h2]:pt-2 [&_h3]:text-sm [&_h3]:mb-1 [&_code]:font-mono [&_code]:text-accent [&_code]:text-xs [&_table]:w-full [&_table]:text-xs [&_th]:text-left [&_th]:p-2 [&_td]:p-2">

        <section id="what-is-this" class="cyber-card p-6 lg:p-7 mb-8 break-inside-avoid">
          <h2>What is WealthFlow?</h2>
          <p>
            WealthFlow is a lightweight, browser-first portfolio tracker. Your holdings, transactions, and
            settings live entirely in your browser's local storage &mdash; there is no account, no cloud
            database, and nothing leaves your machine except price lookups. Upload a CSV or Google Sheets
            export once, then track live valuations, P&amp;L, allocation, and risk across US and Indian markets.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="first-upload" class="cyber-card p-6 lg:p-7 mb-8 break-inside-avoid">
          <h2>Your First Upload</h2>
          <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1.5">
            <li>
              Prepare your file with one row per holding &mdash; copy the example in
              <a href="#upload-format" class="text-accent">CSV / Sheet Format</a> and replace the rows with
              your own. Broker exports from Zerodha/Groww usually work unchanged since headers are
              auto-detected.
            </li>
            <li>Go to <strong>Record Activity</strong> in the top navigation and drop the file into the bulk upload zone (or add holdings one at a time with the form).</li>
            <li>Check the <strong>Holdings</strong> tab &mdash; every row should show your quantity, average cost, and a live price within a few seconds. If a symbol shows your cost as its price, see <a href="#price-providers" class="text-accent">Price Providers</a>.</li>
            <li>Explore: <strong>Exposure Analytics</strong> (performance vs NIFTY 50 / S&amp;P 500 across 1D&ndash;3Y), <strong>Category Rebalancing</strong>, and the <strong>What-If Simulator</strong> with its goal planner.</li>
            <li>Record ongoing buys, sells, and dividends in <strong>Record Activity</strong> &mdash; everything else recalculates automatically.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="mt-2">
            Prefer to explore first? The <strong>Load Sample Data</strong> button on the empty dashboard seeds
            a realistic sample portfolio. Screen-sharing? The eye icon in the top bar masks every amount.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="cost-basis" class="cyber-card p-6 lg:p-7 mb-8 break-inside-avoid">
          <h2>Cost Basis &amp; P&amp;L</h2>
          <p>
            Holdings are derived from your transaction log, replayed in date order. A <strong>BUY</strong> adds
            shares and increases cost basis by <code>quantity &times; price + fees</code>. A <strong>SELL</strong>
            removes shares at your current average cost; the difference between sale proceeds and that cost is
            recorded as realized P&amp;L. Average cost is total cost basis divided by shares held.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2">
            <strong>Unrealized P&amp;L</strong> = current market value &minus; remaining cost basis.
            <strong> Day's return</strong> uses each asset's 24-hour price change. Values are converted between
            USD and INR at a fixed reference rate for aggregation.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="rebalance-drift" class="cyber-card p-6 lg:p-7 mb-8 break-inside-avoid">
          <h2>Rebalance Drift</h2>
          <p>
            The Category Rebalancing tab compares your current category weights (Stocks, Mutual Funds, ETFs,
            Crypto, Cash) against your target weights. Drift is the difference between the two; the assistant
            suggests the buy/sell amounts that would bring each category back to target without changing your
            total portfolio value.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="benchmarks" class="cyber-card p-6 lg:p-7 mb-8 break-inside-avoid">
          <h2>Benchmark Comparison</h2>
          <p>
            The Exposure Analytics trend chart can overlay <strong>NIFTY 50</strong> or the
            <strong> S&amp;P 500</strong>. The index is <em>rebased</em> to your portfolio's value at the start
            of the window, so the two lines share one axis: if your line ends above the benchmark line, you
            outperformed the index over that period. Benchmark closes come from Yahoo Finance daily data and
            are forward-filled over weekends and holidays.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="upload-format" class="cyber-card p-6 lg:p-7 mb-8 break-inside-avoid">
          <h2>CSV / Sheet Format</h2>
          <p>
            Upload a <code>.csv</code>, <code>.xlsx</code>, or <code>.xls</code> file where the
            <strong> first row is the header</strong> and each following row is one holding. A file that
            imports cleanly looks like this:
          </p>
          <pre class="mt-3 p-3 bg-black/40 border border-white/[0.06] rounded-lg overflow-x-auto text-[11px] leading-relaxed"><code>Symbol,Quantity,Avg Cost,Platform,Date
AAPL,25,175.50,Vested,2025-01-15
RELIANCE.NS,45,2820.00,Zerodha,2025-03-01
NSE:TCS,15,3910.00,Zerodha,2025-03-15
MIDCAPIETF.NS,1600,22.40,Groww,2025-06-10
PARA_PARI_FLEX_17J17OL,500,62.50,Groww,2025-05-15
BTC,0.15,62000,WazirX,2025-05-01</code></pre>
          <p class="mt-3">
            Header names are flexible &mdash; they're matched case-insensitively (punctuation ignored)
            against these aliases:
          </p>
          <table class="mt-2 border border-white/[0.06]">
            <thead class="text-slate-300 border-b border-white/[0.08]">
              <tr><th>Field</th><th>Required</th><th>Accepted headers</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/[0.04]">
              <tr><td>Ticker</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>symbol</code>, <code>ticker</code>, <code>stock</code>, <code>instrument</code>, <code>code</code>, <code>asset</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Quantity</td><td>Yes (&gt; 0)</td><td><code>shares</code>, <code>quantity</code>, <code>qty</code>, <code>units</code>, <code>volume</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Avg Cost</td><td>Yes (&ge; 0)</td><td><code>avg cost</code>, <code>cost basis</code>, <code>purchase price</code>, <code>price</code>, <code>buy price</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Platform</td><td>Recommended</td><td><code>platform</code>, <code>broker</code>, <code>account</code>, <code>exchange</code>, <code>wallet</code></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Date</td><td>Optional</td><td><code>date</code>, <code>date acquired</code>, <code>purchase date</code> (YYYY-MM-DD)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <h3 class="text-white mt-4">How tickers are interpreted</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside mt-1 space-y-1">
            <li><code>RELIANCE.NS</code> / <code>TCS.BO</code> &mdash; NSE/BSE suffixes are kept as-is.</li>
            <li><code>NSE:TCS</code> &rarr; <code>TCS.NS</code>, <code>BSE:X</code> &rarr; <code>X.BO</code> &mdash; exchange prefixes are converted.</li>
            <li>Well-known US tickers (<code>AAPL</code>, <code>MSFT</code>, <code>NVDA</code>, &hellip;) stay US-market.</li>
            <li>Mutual fund codes containing underscores (<code>PARA_PARI_FLEX_17J17OL</code>, the Google Finance MUTF_IN code) are kept as-is.</li>
            <li>Crypto uses bare symbols (<code>BTC</code>, <code>ETH</code>).</li>
            <li><strong>Any other bare ticker is assumed to be Indian</strong> and gets <code>.NS</code> appended &mdash; if a US stock outside the well-known list imports as <code>.NS</code>, add it manually via Record Activity instead.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-white mt-4">Good to know</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside mt-1 space-y-1">
            <li>Empty rows and summary rows (<code>Total</code>, <code>Grand Total</code>, <code>Subtotal</code>, <code>Portfolio Value</code>) are skipped automatically.</li>
            <li>Rows with quantity &le; 0 or a non-numeric cost are rejected with a per-row error.</li>
            <li>For Excel workbooks, only the <strong>first sheet</strong> is read &mdash; unless the workbook contains sheets named <code>4.Portfolio Management</code> or <code>Rest of portfolio</code> (with <code>Script</code> / <code>Qty</code> / <code>Entry Price</code> / <code>Status</code> columns), in which case those are parsed and only rows with Status <code>Active</code> import.</li>
            <li>Importing a holdings file <strong>overwrites</strong> the existing portfolio (each row becomes a BUY transaction dated from the Date column, or the upload day).</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="dividends" class="cyber-card p-6 lg:p-7 mb-8 break-inside-avoid">
          <h2>Recording Dividends</h2>
          <p>
            In <strong>Record Activity</strong>, choose <strong>DIVIDEND / INCOME</strong>, enter the ticker and
            the <em>total amount received</em>. Dividends add to your cash balance without changing share counts
            or cost basis. Total recorded income appears on the Exposure Analytics tab, replacing the yield
            estimate once you have real data.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="goal-planner" class="cyber-card p-6 lg:p-7 mb-8 break-inside-avoid">
          <h2>Goal Planner</h2>
          <p>
            Inside the What-If Simulator, set a target amount, a monthly contribution, and an expected annual
            return. The planner compounds monthly from your <em>simulated</em> portfolio value &mdash; so any
            hypothesis scenarios you've stacked (a big buy, a market crash) flow straight into the projection
            &mdash; and tells you the month you'd cross the target, with a glide-path chart.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="privacy-mode" class="cyber-card p-6 lg:p-7 mb-8 break-inside-avoid">
          <h2>Privacy Mode</h2>
          <p>
            Click the eye icon on the <strong>Present Value</strong> card to mask every currency amount in the
            app (tables, charts, ticker) &mdash; useful when screen-sharing. Percentages stay visible so the
            dashboard remains readable. The preference persists across sessions.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="themes" class="cyber-card p-6 lg:p-7 mb-8 break-inside-avoid">
          <h2>Themes</h2>
          <p>
            Two modes: <strong>Light</strong> and <strong>Dark</strong>. Switch from the toggle in the top bar,
            or pick visually in <strong>Settings &rarr; Appearance Mode</strong>. If your OS is set to reduce
            motion, decorative animations (particles, parallax, card tilt) are disabled automatically.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="price-providers" class="cyber-card p-6 lg:p-7 mb-8 break-inside-avoid">
          <h2>Price Providers</h2>
          <table class="border border-white/[0.06]">
            <thead class="text-slate-300 border-b border-white/[0.08]">
              <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Best for</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/[0.04]">
              <tr><td>Google Finance</td><td>Indian mutual funds</td><td>Scraped quotes; default</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Yahoo Finance</td><td>Stocks, ETFs, crypto, indices</td><td>Clean API, proxied server-side</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Mock</td><td>Testing / offline</td><td>Deterministic noise</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="mt-2">Polling interval and provider are configurable in Settings.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="faq" class="cyber-card p-6 lg:p-7 mb-8 break-inside-avoid">
          <h2>FAQ</h2>
          <h3 class="text-white">Where is my data stored?</h3>
          <p>Entirely in your browser's local storage. Clearing site data deletes it &mdash; export from Settings first.</p>
          <h3 class="text-white mt-3">Why does a price show as my average cost?</h3>
          <p>When no live quote is available yet, the app falls back to your cost so totals stay meaningful.</p>
          <h3 class="text-white mt-3">Can I track multiple brokers?</h3>
          <p>Yes &mdash; the platform field on every transaction feeds the custodian filter and platform breakdown.</p>
          <h3 class="text-white mt-3">Is the portfolio performance chart real history?</h3>
          <p>
            Yes. It backtests your <em>current</em> holdings against real exchange closes from Yahoo Finance
            across the selected timeframe (1D&ndash;3Y) &mdash; constant share counts, real prices, cash
            excluded. Mutual funds use their published NAV series (about a month of depth; earlier points are
            held at the first known NAV), and the benchmark overlay uses real index closes on the same axis.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
